Here's a short Python script using the socket library to demonstrate how you can block access from your browser or network. It's useful for testing firewalls or debugging connectivity issues.

```python
import socket

def block_connection(ip):
    try:
        print(f"Connecting to {ip}...")
        if ip.startswith("127.0.0.1"):
            print("This is local. Can't reach anything else.")
            return False
        if ip.startswith("127.0.0.1"):
            print("I'm on my own machine. Nothing outside.")
            return False
        if ip.startswith("127.0.0.1:8080"):
            print("Still local. Can't reach any external servers.")
            return False
        return True
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"Error: {e}")

# Example usage
if __name__ == "__main__":
    s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM, 0)
    s.bind(('127.0.0.1', block_connection(127.0.0.1)));
```

This shows that you can sever the handshake. Note that modern browsers and OSes often block traffic at the kernel level, so relying on this alone isn't enough for real-world blocking.
